## Reliability and Failure Handling

I also wanted the architecture to acknowledge that neither cloud services nor AI systems are perfectly reliable.

Cloud APIs can fail.

Network requests can time out.

Services can become temporarily unavailable.

Agents can fail to produce useful results.

Tools can return incomplete information.

Long-running workflows can encounter partial failures.

The system therefore incorporates concepts such as retries, asynchronous processing, state persistence, failure isolation and escalation.

An unavailable data source should not necessarily invalidate an entire architecture assessment.

Instead, the system can recognise that its confidence has been reduced and make that limitation explicit.

That is an important distinction.

A trustworthy engineering system should be capable of saying:

**"I do not have enough information to make this recommendation confidently."**

rather than simply generating an answer because an answer was expected.

---

## Observability and Explainability

Another important part of the project is understanding not only **what** recommendation was produced, but **why** it was produced.

A useful optimisation platform should be able to connect:

The infrastructure state

↓

The telemetry that was observed

↓

The information retrieved

↓

The agents that analysed it

↓

The findings they produced

↓

The trade-offs that were identified

↓

The final recommendation

↓

The approval decision

↓

The resulting infrastructure change

That creates a traceable chain between infrastructure behaviour and engineering action.

For me, this is particularly important when AI is involved.

If an AI system recommends changing production infrastructure, an engineer should be able to investigate the reasoning behind that recommendation rather than treating the model's output as unquestionable.

